  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Instabridge</strong> if you like the idea of hunting for free WiFi first and only paying for data as a backup, and you're headed somewhere with decent hotspot density. Pick <strong>RedteaGO</strong> if you just want a plain, cheap data eSIM with no app gimmicks, or if you specifically need a plan with calls and texts. Both are budget, data-first providers with public codes rather than an affiliate program, but they solve the "cheap data abroad" problem in genuinely different ways.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: WiFi-Sharing App vs Straight eSIM Store</h2>
  <p>
    Instabridge didn't start as an eSIM company. Its core product is a database of over 20 million community-submitted WiFi hotspot passwords, free to use with no purchase required, and the eSIM is a bolt-on for when free WiFi isn't around or isn't fast enough. RedteaGO has no equivalent: it's a straight eSIM store, and its entire pitch is starting prices as low as $0.52 for a 37-country Europe plan on its own site, not a companion app or a free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    That difference matters for how each one gets used, not just what it costs. Instabridge only makes sense as a hybrid: check the free hotspot map for your destination first, buy data for the gaps. RedteaGO is a normal one-and-done eSIM purchase, no app-hunting involved.
  </p>

